<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no" ?><library hasTree="1" revision="R2023b"><block_node SID="2725" hasTree="1"><block_name>CAN</block_name><block_class></block_class><block_desc></block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/CAN</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/CAN</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>1</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>-330 -536 -230 -494</block_position><block_icon>CAN.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>NA.NA:0</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference><block_node SID="3212" hasTree="0"><block_name>CAN 2.0B</block_name><block_class></block_class><block_desc></block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/CAN/CAN 2.0B</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/CAN/CAN 2.0B</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>1</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>25   24  125   66</block_position><block_icon>CAN/CAN@20@2@2E@0B.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>NA.NA:0</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference><block_node SID="4135" hasTree="0"><block_name>CAN send</block_name><block_class>CAN send (Legacy)</block_class><block_desc>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;CAN send (Legacy)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
Send a message on the CAN channel.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
&lt;b&gt;!! Legacy block — use CAN Send-msg in new projects !!&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;
These blocks will not work together with the -msg blocks in the same model.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Parameters&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Controller type:&lt;/b&gt; Auto-detected from model configuration. Updates pinout labels and CAN channel options.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;CAN channel:&lt;/b&gt; The CAN channel to send the message on.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Identifier type:&lt;/b&gt; Standard (11-bit) or Extended (29-bit) identifier.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Input ports:&lt;/b&gt; The number of input data ports.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Remote frame (RTR):&lt;/b&gt; Enable to send a Remote Transmission Request frame.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Sample time:&lt;/b&gt; -1 or divisible by base sample time [seconds].
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Input&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
Port 1: CAN ID (uint32) in hexadecimal, e.g. hex2dec('0a')&lt;br/&gt;
Port 2..N: CAN message data bytes (uint8)
&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/CAN/CAN 2.0B/CAN send</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/CAN/CAN 2.0B/CAN send</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>40   39  145  201</block_position><block_icon>CAN/CAN@20@2@2E@0B/CAN@20@send.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:2</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4134" hasTree="0"><block_name>CAN receive</block_name><block_class>CAN receive (Legacy)</block_class><block_desc>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;CAN receive (Legacy)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
Receive the newest message from the specified CAN channel by CAN ID.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
&lt;b&gt;!! Legacy block — use CAN Receive-msg in new projects !!&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;
These blocks will not work together with the -msg blocks in the same model.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Parameters&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Controller type:&lt;/b&gt; Auto-detected from model configuration. Updates pinout labels and CAN channel options.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;CAN channel:&lt;/b&gt; The CAN channel to receive messages on.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Output ports:&lt;/b&gt; The number of output data ports.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Output datatype:&lt;/b&gt; The datatype of the output ports.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Byte order:&lt;/b&gt; Checked = MSB first, unchecked = LSB first.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Sample time:&lt;/b&gt; -1 or divisible by base sample time [seconds].
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Input&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
Port 1: CAN ID (uint32) in hexadecimal, e.g. hex2dec('0a')
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Output&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
Port 1: New message flag (bool)&lt;br/&gt;
Port 2..N: Message data bytes
&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/CAN/CAN 2.0B/CAN receive</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/CAN/CAN 2.0B/CAN receive</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>185   40  290  200</block_position><block_icon>CAN/CAN@20@2@2E@0B/CAN@20@receive.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:1,signal.out_signal:2</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4133" hasTree="0"><block_name>CAN Send-msg</block_name><block_class>CAN Send</block_class><block_desc>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;CAN Send&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
Send a CAN message.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
The block expects a CAN_MESSAGE object as input. Use the CAN Pack block
to create the CAN_MESSAGE object.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
Channel: Select the CAN channel on which the message will be transmitted.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
Sample Time: Defines the execution rate of the block and therefore the
transmission rate of the CAN message.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;CAN channel:&lt;/b&gt; a CAN bus of the controller itself or, on the Moduline M1, L4 and HMI1, a CAN bus of a Multibus Module: &lt;i&gt;Multibus CAN 1&lt;/i&gt; (connector interface 1, mb_can1), &lt;i&gt;Multibus CAN 2&lt;/i&gt; (interface 3, mb_can2) and &lt;i&gt;Multibus CAN 3&lt;/i&gt; (interface 4, mb_can3). The Moduline S1 has no Multibus CAN channels.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;A Multibus CAN bus is set up on the controller, not in the model:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;1. Set the connector interface of the module to CAN or CAN FD with the tool &lt;b&gt;go-multibus&lt;/b&gt;.&lt;br&gt;2. Set the bitrate, and for CAN FD the data bitrate, with the tool &lt;b&gt;go-can&lt;/b&gt;, for example &lt;i&gt;go-can set mb_can1 bitrate 500000&lt;/i&gt;.&lt;br&gt;Whether a Multibus Module is fitted is not known when the model is built. If the bus is not there when the application starts, a message is printed and the block does nothing. Click &lt;b&gt;Help&lt;/b&gt; for the steps and the pages of the GOcontroll knowledge base.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This block sends and receives classic CAN frames of up to 8 data bytes, also on a bus that is set to CAN FD.&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/CAN/CAN 2.0B/CAN Send-msg</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/CAN/CAN 2.0B/CAN Send-msg</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>15  265  145  345</block_position><block_icon>CAN/CAN@20@2@2E@0B/CAN@20@Send@2D@msg.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4132" hasTree="0"><block_name>CAN Receive-msg</block_name><block_class>CAN Receive</block_class><block_desc>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;CAN Receive&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
Receive messages from the CAN bus.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
The output of this block is a CAN_MESSAGE object. Use the CAN Unpack block
to extract the signal data from the CAN_MESSAGE object.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
A function-call subsystem is triggered each time a new CAN message is received.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
Only one CAN Receive block can be used per CAN channel. This block receives
all messages on the selected bus.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
To filter specific messages, use CAN Unpack blocks inside the triggered
function-call subsystem.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;CAN channel:&lt;/b&gt; a CAN bus of the controller itself or, on the Moduline M1, L4 and HMI1, a CAN bus of a Multibus Module: &lt;i&gt;Multibus CAN 1&lt;/i&gt; (connector interface 1, mb_can1), &lt;i&gt;Multibus CAN 2&lt;/i&gt; (interface 3, mb_can2) and &lt;i&gt;Multibus CAN 3&lt;/i&gt; (interface 4, mb_can3). The Moduline S1 has no Multibus CAN channels.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;A Multibus CAN bus is set up on the controller, not in the model:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;1. Set the connector interface of the module to CAN or CAN FD with the tool &lt;b&gt;go-multibus&lt;/b&gt;.&lt;br&gt;2. Set the bitrate, and for CAN FD the data bitrate, with the tool &lt;b&gt;go-can&lt;/b&gt;, for example &lt;i&gt;go-can set mb_can1 bitrate 500000&lt;/i&gt;.&lt;br&gt;Whether a Multibus Module is fitted is not known when the model is built. If the bus is not there when the application starts, a message is printed and the block does nothing. Click &lt;b&gt;Help&lt;/b&gt; for the steps and the pages of the GOcontroll knowledge base.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This block sends and receives classic CAN frames of up to 8 data bytes, also on a bus that is set to CAN FD.&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/CAN/CAN 2.0B/CAN Receive-msg</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/CAN/CAN 2.0B/CAN Receive-msg</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>185  265  315  345</block_position><block_icon>CAN/CAN@20@2@2E@0B/CAN@20@Receive@2D@msg.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.out_signal:2</port_domains><block_type>S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4343" hasTree="0"><block_name>CAN configuration</block_name><block_class>CAN configuration</block_class><block_desc>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;CAN Interface Configuration&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
Configure the CAN interface on the Moduline S1
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
Auto retransmit enables automatic retry of a CAN message until it is
successfully transmitted. If disabled, a message that loses arbitration
or encounters an interruption may not be sent.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
This setting are applied to the selected CAN bus.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
The CAN transmit and receive buffer sizes define how many messages can
be stored in the respective queues.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
If the buffer size is too small, messages may be lost and not transmitted
or received correctly.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
This block has no effect on Linux-based targets.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;For the Moduline S1 only.&lt;/b&gt; On the Linux controllers the bitrate of a CAN bus is set on the controller itself.&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/CAN/CAN 2.0B/CAN configuration</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/CAN/CAN 2.0B/CAN configuration</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>115  419  235  491</block_position><block_icon>CAN/CAN@20@2@2E@0B/CAN@20@configuration.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>NA.NA:0</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node></block_node><block_node SID="4403" hasTree="0"><block_name>CAN FD</block_name><block_class></block_class><block_desc></block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/CAN/CAN FD</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/CAN/CAN FD</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>1</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>25  106  125  148</block_position><block_icon>CAN/CAN@20@FD.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>NA.NA:0</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference><block_node SID="4404" hasTree="0"><block_name>CAN FD Receive</block_name><block_class>CAN FD Receive</block_class><block_desc>&lt;h2&gt;CAN FD Receive&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Receives every frame on one CAN bus, classic CAN and CAN FD. The output is a CAN_FD_MESSAGE_BUS, the message type of the CAN FD Pack and CAN FD Unpack blocks (in this library, next to this block). A function-call subsystem is triggered for each received frame; put CAN FD Unpack blocks in it to select messages and extract their signals.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Only one receive block can be used per CAN bus. A bus uses either the CAN FD blocks or the classic CAN blocks, not both; the CAN FD blocks also handle classic CAN frames.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;CAN channel:&lt;/b&gt; a CAN bus of the controller itself or, on the Moduline M1, L4 and HMI1, a CAN bus of a Multibus Module: &lt;i&gt;Multibus CAN 1&lt;/i&gt; (connector interface 1, mb_can1), &lt;i&gt;Multibus CAN 2&lt;/i&gt; (interface 3, mb_can2) and &lt;i&gt;Multibus CAN 3&lt;/i&gt; (interface 4, mb_can3). The Moduline S1 has no Multibus CAN channels.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;A Multibus CAN bus is set up on the controller, not in the model:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;1. Set the connector interface of the module to CAN or CAN FD with the tool &lt;b&gt;go-multibus&lt;/b&gt;.&lt;br&gt;2. Set the bitrate, and for CAN FD the data bitrate, with the tool &lt;b&gt;go-can&lt;/b&gt;, for example &lt;i&gt;go-can set mb_can1 bitrate 500000&lt;/i&gt;.&lt;br&gt;Whether a Multibus Module is fitted is not known when the model is built. If the bus is not there when the application starts, a message is printed and the block does nothing. Click &lt;b&gt;Help&lt;/b&gt; for the steps and the pages of the GOcontroll knowledge base.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;CAN FD frames need a bus that is set to CAN FD. On a classic CAN bus the block works for classic frames only.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;For the Linux controllers (Moduline M1, L4, HMI1) only.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/CAN/CAN FD/CAN FD Receive</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/CAN/CAN FD/CAN FD Receive</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>60   60  190  140</block_position><block_icon>CAN/CAN@20@FD/CAN@20@FD@20@Receive.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.out_signal:2</port_domains><block_type>S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4405" hasTree="0"><block_name>CAN FD Send</block_name><block_class>CAN FD Send</block_class><block_desc>&lt;h2&gt;CAN FD Send&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Sends the CAN_FD_MESSAGE_BUS on its input, made with a CAN FD Pack block (in this library, next to this block). The Protocol mode of the message decides the frame: CAN gives a classic CAN frame of up to 8 data bytes, CAN FD gives a CAN FD frame of up to 64 data bytes, with the bit rate switch when BRS is set in the CAN FD Pack block. A length between two valid CAN FD lengths is rounded up and padded with zeros.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The sample time is the transmit rate. A bus uses either the CAN FD blocks or the classic CAN blocks, not both.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;CAN channel:&lt;/b&gt; a CAN bus of the controller itself or, on the Moduline M1, L4 and HMI1, a CAN bus of a Multibus Module: &lt;i&gt;Multibus CAN 1&lt;/i&gt; (connector interface 1, mb_can1), &lt;i&gt;Multibus CAN 2&lt;/i&gt; (interface 3, mb_can2) and &lt;i&gt;Multibus CAN 3&lt;/i&gt; (interface 4, mb_can3). The Moduline S1 has no Multibus CAN channels.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;A Multibus CAN bus is set up on the controller, not in the model:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;1. Set the connector interface of the module to CAN or CAN FD with the tool &lt;b&gt;go-multibus&lt;/b&gt;.&lt;br&gt;2. Set the bitrate, and for CAN FD the data bitrate, with the tool &lt;b&gt;go-can&lt;/b&gt;, for example &lt;i&gt;go-can set mb_can1 bitrate 500000&lt;/i&gt;.&lt;br&gt;Whether a Multibus Module is fitted is not known when the model is built. If the bus is not there when the application starts, a message is printed and the block does nothing. Click &lt;b&gt;Help&lt;/b&gt; for the steps and the pages of the GOcontroll knowledge base.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;CAN FD frames need a bus that is set to CAN FD. On a classic CAN bus the block works for classic frames only.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;For the Linux controllers (Moduline M1, L4, HMI1) only.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/CAN/CAN FD/CAN FD Send</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/CAN/CAN FD/CAN FD Send</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>260   70  390  130</block_position><block_icon>CAN/CAN@20@FD/CAN@20@FD@20@Send.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4407" hasTree="0"><block_name>CAN FD Unpack</block_name><block_class>CAN FD Unpack</block_class><block_desc>can:maskStrings:fdunpackDescription</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/CAN/CAN FD/CAN FD Unpack</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/CAN/CAN FD/CAN FD Unpack</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>60  190  280  270</block_position><block_icon>CAN/CAN@20@FD/CAN@20@FD@20@Unpack.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:1,signal.out_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>S-Function</block_type><block_reference>canfdmsglib/CAN FD Unpack</block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4406" hasTree="0"><block_name>CAN FD Pack</block_name><block_class>CAN FD Pack</block_class><block_desc>can:maskStrings:fdpackDescription</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/CAN/CAN FD/CAN FD Pack</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/CAN/CAN FD/CAN FD Pack</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>320  190  540  270</block_position><block_icon>CAN/CAN@20@FD/CAN@20@FD@20@Pack.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:1,signal.out_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>S-Function</block_type><block_reference>canfdmsglib/CAN FD Pack</block_reference></block_node></block_node><block_node SID="4136" hasTree="0"><block_name> CAN Example</block_name><block_class>SubSystem</block_class><block_desc>Simulink:blkprm_prompts:SubsysParameterDialogDescr</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/CAN/ CAN Example</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/CAN/ CAN Example</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>25  134  125  176</block_position><block_icon>CAN/@20@CAN@20@Example.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>NA.NA:0</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node></block_node><block_node SID="4051" hasTree="0"><block_name>LIN</block_name><block_class></block_class><block_desc></block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/LIN</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/LIN</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>1</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>-330 -471 -230 -429</block_position><block_icon>LIN.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>NA.NA:0</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference><block_node SID="4115" hasTree="0"><block_name>LIN Master</block_name><block_class>LIN Master</block_class><block_desc>Function
==========
This block implements the LIN master functionality for communication with LIN 
slaves. The block complies with LIN 1.3 an LIN 2.0. When sending data to a slave 
device, this block will add the data to the LIN bus. When retrieving data from slaves, The block will wait for incoming data over LIN.

Parameters
========== 
Identifier: 
Selects the identifier to communicate with

Direction: 
Request data from a slave device or send data to a slave device

Datalength:
Defines the number of bytes that need to be send to- or requested from the slave 

Checksum: 
Select Classic for checksum based on databytes only. Enhanced for databytes and 
ID byte

Sample time:
Defines the execution interval of the block.

Input
==========
Port 1..8               LIN Data bytes to be send to a slave

Output
==========
Port 1..8               LIN Data bytes that are received from the slave&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;For the Linux controllers (Moduline M1, L4, HMI1) only.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/LIN/LIN Master</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/LIN/LIN Master</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>-30   96   90  354</block_position><block_icon>LIN/LIN@20@Master.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:2</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node></block_node><block_node SID="3908" hasTree="1"><block_name>Network</block_name><block_class></block_class><block_desc></block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Network</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Network</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>1</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>-330 -406 -230 -364</block_position><block_icon>Network.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>NA.NA:0</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference><block_node SID="4229" hasTree="0"><block_name>TCP</block_name><block_class></block_class><block_desc></block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Network/TCP</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Network/TCP</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>1</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>25 -146  125 -104</block_position><block_icon>Network/TCP.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>NA.NA:0</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference><block_node SID="4230" hasTree="0"><block_name>TCP Configure</block_name><block_class>TCP Configure</block_class><block_desc>Block to configure a TCP socket

The socket ID should be a unique identifier to link the send and receive blocks to.
The name of the actual socket in the C code will be socket_&lt;socket id&gt;, so characters that would not work in a C variable name will not work.

Setting the ip to 0.0.0.0 makes the controller accept messages on all interfaces.
Set it to the ip of a specific interface to limit this.

The socket mode determines whether this socket will listen for a connection or actively try to make one to a given ip and port.
Server will wait for something to connect to it, the client will try to connect to a server at the given ip and port

Setting the timeout to zero disables the timeout

When the disconnect input is true, it will close the current connection if there is one, if there isn't one it will not allow a connection to be made as long as it is true.

Outputs whether or not the socket currently has an active connection (true means connnected)&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;For the Linux controllers (Moduline M1, L4, HMI1) only.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Network/TCP/TCP Configure</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Network/TCP/TCP Configure</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>1070   425  1225   495</block_position><block_icon>Network/TCP/TCP@20@Configure.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:1,signal.out_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4272" hasTree="0"><block_name>TCP Receive</block_name><block_class>TCP Receive</block_class><block_desc>Receive all TCP messages on the given socket

For every message received this block will call the functions attached to the function call output.
In these function calls you can then use the message and message length outputs to process/buffer the received messages.

Outputs:
function call:
Attach to a function call subsystem or something like it to call that function every time a message is received.

message:
The uint8 vector received from the socket. The length of this vector is likely longer than the actual number of bytes received.

message length:
The number of bytes actually received, use this to get the actual fresh bytes from the message.

Parameters:
buffer size:
The maximum number of bytes that can be read from the socket in one message, this also determines the width of the message vector.&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;For the Linux controllers (Moduline M1, L4, HMI1) only.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Network/TCP/TCP Receive</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Network/TCP/TCP Receive</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>1070   524  1225   596</block_position><block_icon>Network/TCP/TCP@20@Receive.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.out_signal:3</port_domains><block_type>S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4273" hasTree="0"><block_name>TCP Send</block_name><block_class>TCP Send</block_class><block_desc>Send a TCP message to the chosen sockets connection, whether it be client or server.

The Message input should be a uint8 vector, if you end up with a [1xn] or [nx1] matrix, use the reshape block to turn it into a vector.&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;For the Linux controllers (Moduline M1, L4, HMI1) only.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Network/TCP/TCP Send</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Network/TCP/TCP Send</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>1070   627  1225   703</block_position><block_icon>Network/TCP/TCP@20@Send.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node></block_node><block_node SID="4065" hasTree="0"><block_name>UDP</block_name><block_class></block_class><block_desc></block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Network/UDP</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Network/UDP</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>1</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>25  -61  125  -19</block_position><block_icon>Network/UDP.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>NA.NA:0</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference><block_node SID="4091" hasTree="0"><block_name>UDP Configure</block_name><block_class>UDP Configure</block_class><block_desc>Block to configure a UDP connection

The socket ID should be a unique identifier to link the send and receive blocks to.
The name of the actual socket in the C code will be socket_&lt;socket id&gt;, so characters that would not work in a C variable name will not work.

Setting the ip to 0.0.0.0 makes the controller accept messages on all interfaces.
Set it to the ip of a specific interface to limit this.

Allowing broadcasting enables you to send messages to a full range of ip addresses, for example:
192.168.255.255 sends messages to 192.168.1.1, 192.168.2.1 ... 192.168.1.2, 192.168.1.3...&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;For the Linux controllers (Moduline M1, L4, HMI1) only.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Network/UDP/UDP Configure</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Network/UDP/UDP Configure</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>1070   425  1225   495</block_position><block_icon>Network/UDP/UDP@20@Configure.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>NA.NA:0</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4092" hasTree="0"><block_name>UDP Receive</block_name><block_class>UDP Receive</block_class><block_desc>Receive all UDP messages

For every message received this block will call the functions attached to the function call output.
In these function calls you can then use the message and message length outputs to process/buffer the received messages.

Outputs:
function call:
Attach to a function call subsystem or something like it to call that function every time a message is received.

message:
The uint8 vector received from the socket. The length of this vector is likely longer than the actual number of bytes received.

message length:
The number of bytes actually received, use this to get the actual fresh bytes from the message.

Parameters:
buffer size:
The maximum number of bytes that can be read from the socket in one message, this also determines the width of the message vector.&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;For the Linux controllers (Moduline M1, L4, HMI1) only.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Network/UDP/UDP Receive</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Network/UDP/UDP Receive</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>1070   524  1225   596</block_position><block_icon>Network/UDP/UDP@20@Receive.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.out_signal:3</port_domains><block_type>S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4093" hasTree="0"><block_name>UDP Send</block_name><block_class>UDP Send</block_class><block_desc>Send a UDP message to the designated address.

The Message input should be a uint8 vector, if you end up with a [1xn] or [nx1] matrix, use the reshape block to turn it into a vector.

IP address should be a valid IP address as a string

Port should be a within the range of a uint16&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;For the Linux controllers (Moduline M1, L4, HMI1) only.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Network/UDP/UDP Send</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Network/UDP/UDP Send</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>1070   627  1225   703</block_position><block_icon>Network/UDP/UDP@20@Send.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4175" hasTree="0"><block_name>UDP Example</block_name><block_class>SubSystem</block_class><block_desc>Simulink:blkprm_prompts:SubsysParameterDialogDescr</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Network/UDP/UDP Example</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Network/UDP/UDP Example</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>1070   734  1225   816</block_position><block_icon>Network/UDP/UDP@20@Example.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>NA.NA:0</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node></block_node><block_node SID="3909" hasTree="0"><block_name>MQTT</block_name><block_class></block_class><block_desc></block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Network/MQTT</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Network/MQTT</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>1</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>25   24  125   66</block_position><block_icon>Network/MQTT.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>NA.NA:0</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference><block_node SID="4346" hasTree="0"><block_name>MQTT Broker</block_name><block_class>MQTT Broker</block_class><block_desc>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;MQTT Broker&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
Configures the connection to an MQTT broker. This block is required in every model
that uses MQTT Publish or MQTT Subscribe blocks.
Only one MQTT Broker block may be used per model.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Settings on the controller&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
The broker URL, port, user and password, and the SIM pin code, can also be set on the
Moduline S1 itself during commissioning (GOcontroll Deployment Tool). Every field you leave
empty here (port: 0) is taken from those controller settings, so the application never has
to contain credentials. A value filled in here takes precedence over the controller setting.
User and password go together: fill in both here, or leave both empty.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Parameters&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Provider APN&lt;/b&gt; — access point name of the mobile data provider (e.g. super, internet).&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;SIM pin code&lt;/b&gt; — PIN code of the SIM card. Leave empty to use the PIN set on the controller (default 0000). The controller enters it only when the SIM asks for a PIN, and never retries a PIN the SIM refused.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Client&lt;/b&gt; — unique client identifier for this device on the broker. Leave empty to use the controller serial number.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Broker URL&lt;/b&gt; — hostname or IP address of the MQTT broker, optionally prefixed mqtt:// or mqtts://. Leave empty to use the broker set on the controller.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Broker port&lt;/b&gt; — TCP port of the MQTT broker. 0 = the port set on the controller; without one 1883 (8883 for mqtts://).&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;User&lt;/b&gt; — username for broker authentication. Leave empty to use the user and password set on the controller.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Password&lt;/b&gt; — password for broker authentication, used together with User.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Time to live (s)&lt;/b&gt; — keep-alive interval in seconds. The controller sends a ping to the broker at this interval to maintain the connection.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;For the Moduline S1 only.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Network/MQTT/MQTT Broker</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Network/MQTT/MQTT Broker</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>330  232  485  308</block_position><block_icon>Network/MQTT/MQTT@20@Broker.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>NA.NA:0</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4347" hasTree="0"><block_name>MQTT Publish</block_name><block_class>MQTT Publish</block_class><block_desc>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;MQTT Publish&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
Publishes a payload to an MQTT topic each model step.
Requires an MQTT Broker block in the model.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Input ports&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Topic&lt;/b&gt; — uint8 array (null-terminated string, max 64 characters). The topic to publish to.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Payload&lt;/b&gt; — any data type and width. The raw bytes to publish.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Len&lt;/b&gt; (optional) — uint16 scalar. Actual byte count to publish.
Only present when &lt;i&gt;Payload length&lt;/i&gt; is set to &lt;b&gt;From port&lt;/b&gt;.
Connect to the &lt;b&gt;Len&lt;/b&gt; output of the JSON Constructor block.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Parameters&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Sample time&lt;/b&gt; — model sample time in seconds.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Payload length&lt;/b&gt; — &lt;b&gt;Auto&lt;/b&gt;: use sizeof(payload type) × signal width (suitable for typed signals).
&lt;b&gt;From port&lt;/b&gt;: use the value on the &lt;i&gt;Len&lt;/i&gt; input port (suitable for variable-length payloads such as JSON strings).&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Quality of service&lt;/b&gt; — MQTT QoS level: 0 (at most once), 1 (at least once), or 2 (exactly once).&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Retain&lt;/b&gt; — when set to Retain message, the broker stores the last message and delivers it to new subscribers immediately upon connection.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;For the Moduline S1 only.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Network/MQTT/MQTT Publish</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Network/MQTT/MQTT Publish</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>330  342  485  418</block_position><block_icon>Network/MQTT/MQTT@20@Publish.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:2</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4349" hasTree="0"><block_name>MQTT Subscribe</block_name><block_class>MQTT Subscribe</block_class><block_desc>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;MQTT Subscribe&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
Subscribes to an MQTT topic and outputs the received payload each model step.
Requires an MQTT Broker block in the model.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Input ports&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Topic&lt;/b&gt; — uint8 array (null-terminated string, max 64 characters).
Read once at model initialisation to set the subscription topic.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Output ports&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
&lt;b&gt;New data&lt;/b&gt; — uint8 scalar, 1 if a new message has arrived since the last step, 0 otherwise.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Length&lt;/b&gt; — uint16 scalar, number of valid bytes in the last received message.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Payload&lt;/b&gt; — uint8 vector, length set by the Payload size parameter.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Parameters&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Sample time&lt;/b&gt; — model sample time in seconds.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Payload size&lt;/b&gt; — maximum payload length in bytes; sets the width of the Payload output port.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Quality of service&lt;/b&gt; — MQTT QoS level: 0 (at most once), 1 (at least once), or 2 (exactly once).
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;For the Moduline S1 only.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Network/MQTT/MQTT Subscribe</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Network/MQTT/MQTT Subscribe</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>330  452  485  528</block_position><block_icon>Network/MQTT/MQTT@20@Subscribe.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:1,signal.out_signal:3</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4400" hasTree="0"><block_name>JSON Constructor</block_name><block_class>JSON Constructor</block_class><block_desc>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;JSON Constructor&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
Builds a JSON object string from N scalar input signals.
The resulting byte array is suitable for connecting directly to the
&lt;b&gt;MQTT Publish&lt;/b&gt; payload input.
Moduline S1 target only.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Input ports&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
One port per configured signal (1–8).
Each port accepts any scalar datatype (float, double, int8…int32, uint8…uint32).
The port label shows the configured JSON key name.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Output ports&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
&lt;b&gt;JSON&lt;/b&gt; — uint8 array of length &lt;i&gt;Buffer size&lt;/i&gt;.
Contains the raw JSON bytes, e.g. &lt;tt&gt;{"temp":23.4,"druk":230}&lt;/tt&gt;.
Connect to the &lt;b&gt;MQTT Publish&lt;/b&gt; payload input.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Len&lt;/b&gt; — uint16 scalar.
Actual byte count of the JSON string (excluding null terminator).
Connect to the &lt;b&gt;MQTT Publish&lt;/b&gt; length input (if available).
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Parameters&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Number of signals&lt;/b&gt; — integer 1–8; sets the number of input ports and JSON fields.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Key 1 … Key N&lt;/b&gt; — JSON key name for each input signal.
Only the first &lt;i&gt;Number of signals&lt;/i&gt; keys are used.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Sample time&lt;/b&gt; — model sample time in seconds.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Buffer size (bytes)&lt;/b&gt; — size of the output byte array.
Must be larger than the longest possible JSON string.
Default: 256.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;For the Moduline S1 only.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Network/MQTT/JSON Constructor</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Network/MQTT/JSON Constructor</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>330  562  485  638</block_position><block_icon>Network/MQTT/JSON@20@Constructor.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:2,signal.out_signal:2</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4402" hasTree="0"><block_name>JSON Parser</block_name><block_class>JSON Parser</block_class><block_desc>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;JSON Parser&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
Parses an incoming JSON object string received from an &lt;b&gt;MQTT Subscribe&lt;/b&gt; block
and outputs the numeric value for each configured key as a separate port.
Moduline S1 target only.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Input ports&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
&lt;b&gt;new&lt;/b&gt; — uint8 scalar. New-data flag from MQTT Subscribe output 1.
Parsing only occurs when this is 1.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;len&lt;/b&gt; — uint16 scalar. Payload length from MQTT Subscribe output 2.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;payload&lt;/b&gt; — uint8 array. Payload bytes from MQTT Subscribe output 3.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Output ports&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
One &lt;b&gt;real_T&lt;/b&gt; (double) scalar per configured key.
The port label shows the key name.
If a key is absent from the incoming JSON message the output retains its previous value.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Parameters&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Number of keys&lt;/b&gt; — integer 1–8; sets the number of output ports and JSON keys to search for.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Key 1 … Key N&lt;/b&gt; — JSON key name to extract from the incoming message.
Only the first &lt;i&gt;Number of keys&lt;/i&gt; fields are used.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Sample time&lt;/b&gt; — model sample time in seconds.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Buffer size (bytes)&lt;/b&gt; — internal parse buffer size.
Should match the payload_size of the connected MQTT Subscribe block.
Default: 256.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;For the Moduline S1 only.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Network/MQTT/JSON Parser</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Network/MQTT/JSON Parser</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>330  672  485  748</block_position><block_icon>Network/MQTT/JSON@20@Parser.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:3,signal.out_signal:2</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node></block_node></block_node><block_node SID="3919" hasTree="1"><block_name>Modules</block_name><block_class></block_class><block_desc></block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Modules</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Modules</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>1</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>-330 -336 -230 -294</block_position><block_icon>Modules.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>NA.NA:0</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference><block_node SID="3989" hasTree="0"><block_name>Input Module</block_name><block_class></block_class><block_desc></block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Modules/Input Module</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Modules/Input Module</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>1</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>290 -236  400 -194</block_position><block_icon>Modules/Input@20@Module.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>NA.NA:0</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference><block_node SID="4108" hasTree="0"><block_name>Input Module (6 channel)</block_name><block_class>Input Module</block_class><block_desc>With this function block, you can configure each channel of the 6 channel input module (201001xx). The configuration of the module is set once at initialization. During runtime, the block outputs the measured values from each channel.

Module location:
Sets the location of the module slot where the module is physically 
plugged into.

Channel tabs:
Each channel has its own configuration tab.

Function select:
Select the desired input signal configuration.

Voltage range:
Select the expected input range of the signal.

Analog filter samples:
The number of samples that are passed into a moving average filter. 
(only active in case of an analog input)

Pulses per rotation
The number of pulses for one rotation. This parameter is needed in case the
module needs to calculate the rotation speed from i.e. an axle.

Pull Up:
If required, a pull-up resistance can be added to the input signal.

Pull Down:
If required, a pull-down resistance can be added to the input signal.</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Modules/Input Module/Input Module (6 channel)</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Modules/Input Module/Input Module (6 channel)</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>205   92  390  183</block_position><block_icon>Modules/Input@20@Module/Input@20@Module@20@@28@6@20@channel@29@.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.out_signal:6</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4109" hasTree="0"><block_name>Input Module (10 channel)</block_name><block_class>Input Module</block_class><block_desc>With this block, you can configure each channel of the 10 channel input module (201002xx). The configuration of the module is set once at initialization. During runtime, the block outputs the measured values from each channel. 

Module location:
Sets the location of the module slot where the module is physically 
plugged into.

Channel tabs:
Each channel has its own configuration tab.

Function select:
Select the desired input signal configuration.

Pull Up:
If required, a pull-up resistance can be added to the input signal.

Pull Down:
If required, a pull-down resistance can be added to the input signal.</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Modules/Input Module/Input Module (10 channel)</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Modules/Input Module/Input Module (10 channel)</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>205  216  390  379</block_position><block_icon>Modules/Input@20@Module/Input@20@Module@20@@28@10@20@channel@29@.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.out_signal:10</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4110" hasTree="0"><block_name>Reset Pulse Counter (6 channel)</block_name><block_class>Reset pulse counter</block_class><block_desc>This block resets the pulsecounter from a specified input channel of the input module that is configured as a pulsecounter. A trigger on input pin 1 wil set the pulsecounter to the value applied on pin 2 </block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Modules/Input Module/Reset Pulse Counter (6 channel)</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Modules/Input Module/Reset Pulse Counter (6 channel)</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>205  415  390  470</block_position><block_icon>Modules/Input@20@Module/Reset@20@Pulse@20@Counter@20@@28@6@20@channel@29@.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:2</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4111" hasTree="0"><block_name>Input Module 4-20 mA (10 channel)</block_name><block_class>4-20 mA Input Module</block_class><block_desc>Controller type:
Selects the controller that is to be programmed. Pinning on the mask will be adopted accordingly.

Module slot:
Sets the location of the module slot where the module is physically plugged into.

Supply for different channels:
Enables the 16V supply per 2 channels. When some input pins are not used, it is recommended to disable these inputs to prevent controller pins floating at 16V.

Module status:
The status output provides information of the actual state per 2 channels:

0x00 all channels are supplied no problems.
0x01 Channel 1 &amp; 2 over current detected and supply disconnected.
0x02 Channel 3 &amp; 4 over current detected and supply disconnected.
0x04 Channel 5 &amp; 6 over current detected and supply disconnected.
0x08 Channel 7 &amp; 8 over current detected and supply disconnected.
0x10 Channel 9 &amp; 10 over current detected and supply disconnected.
</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Modules/Input Module/Input Module 4-20 mA (10 channel)</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Modules/Input Module/Input Module 4-20 mA (10 channel)</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>205  509  390  671</block_position><block_icon>Modules/Input@20@Module/Input@20@Module@20@4@2D@20@20@mA@20@@28@10@20@channel@29@.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.out_signal:11</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node></block_node><block_node SID="3990" hasTree="0"><block_name>Output Module</block_name><block_class></block_class><block_desc></block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Modules/Output Module</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Modules/Output Module</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>1</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>290 -171  400 -129</block_position><block_icon>Modules/Output@20@Module.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>NA.NA:0</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference><block_node SID="4105" hasTree="0"><block_name>Bridge Module</block_name><block_class>Bridge Module</block_class><block_desc>With this function block, you can configure each output of the bridge module (202001xx). During initialization of the controller, the corresponding outputs of the module are set according the settings. During runtime, the value pushed into the block will control the outputs.

Module slot:
Sets the location of the module slot where the module is physically 
plugged into.

Channel tabs:
Each ouput has its own configuration tab.

Function select:
Select the desired output configuration.

Frequency:
In case a duty cycle is used, select the frequency.
</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Modules/Output Module/Bridge Module</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Modules/Output Module/Bridge Module</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>610   80  855  155</block_position><block_icon>Modules/Output@20@Module/Bridge@20@Module.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:2</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4107" hasTree="0"><block_name>Bridge Module Monitor</block_name><block_class>Bridge Module Monitor</block_class><block_desc>With this function block, you can read parameters from the specified bridge module (202001xx). The block will provide some information about the module such as:

Module temperature:
The temperature in degrees from the output module.

Module ground shift
In case the ground connection of the module is poor, the power ground is shifted. The value gives the ground difference in mV and schould be beneath 50 mV

Channel current
Current related output for each output channel
</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Modules/Output Module/Bridge Module Monitor</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Modules/Output Module/Bridge Module Monitor</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>610  176  855  254</block_position><block_icon>Modules/Output@20@Module/Bridge@20@Module@20@Monitor.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.out_signal:4</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4112" hasTree="0"><block_name>Output Module (6 channel)</block_name><block_class>6 Channel Output Module</block_class><block_desc>With this block, you can configure each output of the 6 channel output module (202002xx). During initialization of the controller, the corresponding outputs of the module are set according the settings. During runtime, the value pushed into the block will control the outputs.

Sample time:
Execution frequency from function block. For proper functioning of the output module, us a sampletime of 100 ms or lower.

Module slot:
Sets the location of the module slot where the module is physically plugged into.

Frequency:
In case a duty cycle is used, select the frequency.

Function select:
Select the desired output configuration for each channel
</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Modules/Output Module/Output Module (6 channel)</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Modules/Output Module/Output Module (6 channel)</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>625  275  840  380</block_position><block_icon>Modules/Output@20@Module/Output@20@Module@20@@28@6@20@channel@29@.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:6</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4310" hasTree="0"><block_name>Output Module (10 channel)</block_name><block_class>10 Channel Output Module</block_class><block_desc>With this function block, you can configure each output of the 10 channel output module (202003xx). During initialization of the controller, the corresponding outputs of the module are set according the settings. During runtime, the value pushed into the block will control the outputs.

Sample time:
Execution frequency from function block. For proper functioning of the output module, use a sampletime of 100 ms or lower.

Module slot:
Sets the location of the module slot where the module is physically plugged into.

Frequency:
In case a duty cycle is used, select the frequency.

Function select:
Select the desired output configuration for each channel
</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Modules/Output Module/Output Module (10 channel)</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Modules/Output Module/Output Module (10 channel)</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>625  399  840  566</block_position><block_icon>Modules/Output@20@Module/Output@20@Module@20@@28@10@20@channel@29@.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:10</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4114" hasTree="0"><block_name>Output Module Monitor (6 channel)</block_name><block_class>Output Module Monitor</block_class><block_desc>With this function block, you can read parameters from the specified 6 channel output module (202002xx). The block will provide some information about the module such as:

Module temperature:
The temperature in degrees from the output module.

Module ground shift
In case the ground connection of the module is poor, the power ground is shifted. The value gives the ground difference in mV and schould be beneath 50 mV

Module supply voltage
The measured supply voltage of the module in mV. This output only works for hardware version 7 and above, gives off 0 otherwise.

Channel current
The current from each channel is measured and given in mA. If the ouput is configured as high side, the result will be positive. If the ouput is configured as low side, the value will be negative

Channel duty cycle
This output only appears if the corresponding output module block has a channel in peak and hold mode, and it only works for module firmware 2.1.0 and higher.
This output can be used to monitor the actual output duty cycle. This value can then be used to perform extra checks on the output in this mode.
</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Modules/Output Module/Output Module Monitor (6 channel)</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Modules/Output Module/Output Module Monitor (6 channel)</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>625  583  925  717</block_position><block_icon>Modules/Output@20@Module/Output@20@Module@20@Monitor@20@@28@6@20@channel@29@.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.out_signal:10</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4311" hasTree="0"><block_name>Output Module Monitor (10 channel)</block_name><block_class>Output Module Monitor (10 channel)</block_class><block_desc>With this function block, you can read parameters from the specified 10 channel output module (202003xx). The block will provide some information about the module such as:

Module temperature:
The temperature in degrees from the output module.

Module ground shift
In case the ground connection of the module is poor, the power ground is shifted. The value gives the ground difference in mV and schould be beneath 50 mV

Module supply voltage
The measured supply voltage of the module in mV.

Module error code
If the module encounters an error, the relevant fault bit will be set here. See the software manual on the website: https://gocontroll.com/downloads/

Module current
The total current of the module is measured and given in mA.

</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Modules/Output Module/Output Module Monitor (10 channel)</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Modules/Output Module/Output Module Monitor (10 channel)</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>625  733  925  867</block_position><block_icon>Modules/Output@20@Module/Output@20@Module@20@Monitor@20@@28@10@20@channel@29@.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.out_signal:5</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node></block_node></block_node><block_node SID="3975" hasTree="0"><block_name>Enclosure LED's</block_name><block_class></block_class><block_desc></block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Enclosure LED's</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Enclosure LED's</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>1</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>-330 -266 -230 -224</block_position><block_icon>Enclosure@20@LED@27@s.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>NA.NA:0</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference><block_node SID="4102" hasTree="0"><block_name>Enclosure LED</block_name><block_class>Enclosure LED's</block_class><block_desc>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Status LED Configuration&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
&lt;table border="1" cellpadding="4" cellspacing="0"&gt;
  &lt;tr&gt;
    &lt;td&gt;Moduline L4&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;td&gt;4 RGB LEDs&lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;tr&gt;
    &lt;td&gt;Moduline M1&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;td&gt;4 RGB LEDs&lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;tr&gt;
    &lt;td&gt;Moduline HMI1&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;td&gt;No LEDs&lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;tr&gt;
    &lt;td&gt;Moduline S1&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;td&gt;1 RGB LED&lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Control Method&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
To control the LEDs, configure the mask parameters for LED selection and color. 
The brightness can be controlled with a value from 0 (0%) to 255 (100%) (uint8).
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Mask Parameters&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
LED: Select the LED that needs to be controlled.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
Color: Select the color the LED should illuminate.
&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Enclosure LED's/Enclosure LED</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Enclosure LED's/Enclosure LED</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>-20 -166  130 -124</block_position><block_icon>Enclosure@20@LED@27@s/Enclosure@20@LED.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node></block_node><block_node SID="3980" hasTree="0"><block_name>Supply control</block_name><block_class></block_class><block_desc></block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Supply control</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Supply control</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>1</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>-330 -196 -230 -154</block_position><block_icon>Supply@20@control.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>NA.NA:0</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference><block_node SID="4097" hasTree="0"><block_name>Controller Active</block_name><block_class>Controller Active</block_class><block_desc>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Power Latching &amp; Shutdown Behavior&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
GOcontroll Moduline controllers support an internal power-latching mechanism 
that allows the device to remain powered under software control.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Conditions to Remain Powered&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The controller will remain powered as long as:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;K30 supply voltage is present on the designated K30 power pins&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;The input of the Controller Active function block is set high (boolean)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
Under these conditions, the controller will stay powered even if the 
K15 ignition inputs are no longer active.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Software Shutdown Behavior&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
If the controller is switched off by software (Controller Active = low), 
the device will fully power down.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
A restart is only possible after reactivation via one of the designated 
K15 ignition inputs.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Detecting External Shutdown (Ignition Off)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
To detect when an external shutdown occurs (K15 voltage drops), use the 
K15 Voltage function block in your Simulink model.
&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Supply control/Controller Active</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Supply control/Controller Active</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>110  334  260  376</block_position><block_icon>Supply@20@control/Controller@20@Active.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4095" hasTree="0"><block_name>K15 Voltage</block_name><block_class>Contact Voltage</block_class><block_desc>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;K15 Voltage Measurement&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
The K15 Voltage block provides the measured K15 input voltage in millivolts (mV) 
that is applied to the controller’s K15 ignition pins.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
A positive voltage on any of these pins will initiate controller startup.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Startup Source Identification&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
The available function blocks can be used to determine which specific 
K15 input pin triggered the startup event.
&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Supply control/K15 Voltage</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Supply control/K15 Voltage</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>110  399  260  441</block_position><block_icon>Supply@20@control/K15@20@Voltage.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.out_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4096" hasTree="0"><block_name>K30 Voltage</block_name><block_class>Supply voltage</block_class><block_desc>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;K30 Voltage Measurement&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
The K30 Voltage block provides the measured supply voltage in millivolts (mV) 
that is applied to the controller’s K30 power pins.
&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Supply control/K30 Voltage</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Supply control/K30 Voltage</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>110  464  260  506</block_position><block_icon>Supply@20@control/K30@20@Voltage.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.out_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node></block_node><block_node SID="4007" hasTree="1"><block_name>Memory store</block_name><block_class></block_class><block_desc></block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Memory store</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Memory store</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>1</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>-330 -131 -230  -89</block_position><block_icon>Memory@20@store.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>NA.NA:0</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference><block_node SID="4032" hasTree="0"><block_name>Variable to memory</block_name><block_class></block_class><block_desc></block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Memory store/Variable to memory</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Memory store/Variable to memory</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>1</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>470  439  570  481</block_position><block_icon>Memory@20@store/Variable@20@to@20@memory.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>NA.NA:0</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference><block_node SID="4103" hasTree="0"><block_name>Memory write</block_name><block_class>Store data to memory</block_class><block_desc>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Write to Memory&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
This function block stores a value in non-volatile memory using a specified key.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
To retrieve a stored value, use the Read from Memory function block and
provide the corresponding key.
&lt;/p&gt;
</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Memory store/Variable to memory/Memory write</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Memory store/Variable to memory/Memory write</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>485  511  640  559</block_position><block_icon>Memory@20@store/Variable@20@to@20@memory/Memory@20@write.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4104" hasTree="0"><block_name>Memory read</block_name><block_class>Read value from memory</block_class><block_desc>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Read from Memory&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
This function block reads a value from non-volatile memory using the specified key.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
To store a value in memory, use the Write to Memory function block.
&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Memory store/Variable to memory/Memory read</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Memory store/Variable to memory/Memory read</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>485  585  640  635</block_position><block_icon>Memory@20@store/Variable@20@to@20@memory/Memory@20@read.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.out_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node></block_node><block_node SID="4035" hasTree="1"><block_name>Diagnostic to memory</block_name><block_class></block_class><block_desc></block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Memory store/Diagnostic to memory</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Memory store/Diagnostic to memory</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>1</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>470  519  570  561</block_position><block_icon>Memory@20@store/Diagnostic@20@to@20@memory.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>NA.NA:0</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference><block_node SID="4298" hasTree="0"><block_name>Store diagnostic code</block_name><block_class>Store diagnostic code to memory</block_class><block_desc>This function block stores a diagnostic code to memory.

SPN number:
A code to identify which part has failed

FMI:
How the part has failed</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Memory store/Diagnostic to memory/Store diagnostic code</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Memory store/Diagnostic to memory/Store diagnostic code</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>605  324  840  376</block_position><block_icon>Memory@20@store/Diagnostic@20@to@20@memory/Store@20@diagnostic@20@code.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4338" hasTree="0"><block_name>Diagnostic Helpers</block_name><block_class></block_class><block_desc></block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Memory store/Diagnostic to memory/Diagnostic Helpers</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Memory store/Diagnostic to memory/Diagnostic Helpers</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>1</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>350  454  535  511</block_position><block_icon>Memory@20@store/Diagnostic@20@to@20@memory/Diagnostic@20@Helpers.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>NA.NA:0</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference><block_node SID="4339" hasTree="0"><block_name>Actuator diagnostic</block_name><block_class>Generic actuator diagnostic block</block_class><block_desc>This block executes diagnostics on an actuator that goes into the block. The current during control is checked.

It must be enabled via the enable input at the top to function, this can be a easy way to toggle diagnostics on or off.

To use this block, error limits should be defined in the workspace. Several pre- and suffixes are needed to work with this block. For example:

Base actuator name is: Valve1 (this will be the signal name in the mask)

- Valve1_ACTIVE_DUTY_CYCLE (This is the dutycycle when the valve is controlles. in case a switch is used, use 1)
- Valve1_MIN_CURRENT (Current in mA that should be the minimal to control the valve)
- Valve1_MAX_CURRENT (Current in mA that should be the minimal to control the valve)

Setting Diagnostic Trouble Code (DTC) in memory:

Configure the SPN number corresponding to the signal in the workspace parameters as:
Valve1_SPN

Output provide two uint8 signals in a bus holding the volatile and permanent fault flags.

</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Memory store/Diagnostic to memory/Diagnostic Helpers/Actuator diagnostic</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Memory store/Diagnostic to memory/Diagnostic Helpers/Actuator diagnostic</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>-65  372  240  468</block_position><block_icon>Memory@20@store/Diagnostic@20@to@20@memory/Diagnostic@20@Helpers/Actuator@20@diagnostic.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:4,signal.out_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4342" hasTree="0"><block_name>Signal diagnostic</block_name><block_class>Generic Signal diagnostic block</block_class><block_desc>This block executes diagnostics on a signal that goes into the block. Both sensor voltage and signal thersholds are checked.

It must be enabled via the enable input at the top to function, this can be a easy way to toggle diagnostics on or off.

To use this block, error and warning limits should be defined in the workspace. Several pre- and suffixes are needed to work with this block. For example:

Base signal name is: Coolant_Pt1 (this will be the signal name in the mask)

- Coolant_Pt1_RAW_ERR_LOW (low voltage error)
- Coolant_Pt1_RAW_ERR_HIGH (high voltage error)
- Coolant_Pt1_SIG_WAR_LOW (Low warning signal threshold)
- Coolant_Pt1_SIG_WAR_HIGH (High warning signal threshold)
- Coolant_Pt1_SIG_ERR_LOW (Low error signal threshold)
- Coolant_Pt1 _SIG_ERR_HIGH (High error signal threshold)

Setting Diagnostic Trouble Code (DTC) in memory:

Configure the SPN number corresponding to the signal in the workspace parameters as:
Coolant_Pt1_SPN

Output provide two uint8 signals in a bus holding the volatile and permanent fault flags.

</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Memory store/Diagnostic to memory/Diagnostic Helpers/Signal diagnostic</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Memory store/Diagnostic to memory/Diagnostic Helpers/Signal diagnostic</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>-65  499  240  591</block_position><block_icon>Memory@20@store/Diagnostic@20@to@20@memory/Diagnostic@20@Helpers/Signal@20@diagnostic.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:4,signal.out_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4340" hasTree="0"><block_name>Diagnostic simulation</block_name><block_class>Diagnostic simulation</block_class><block_desc>This block works together with the "Signal adaptation" and "Signal diagnostic" blocks to provide an easy way to test the setting of diagnostic codes for analogue inputs.

It needs both of those blocks to be properly set up in the model along with their workspace variables. Then the Simulation variable of this signal must be set to 1.

A diagnostic scenario can then be injected:
== 1/default: normal operation
== 2: low voltage
== 3: high voltage
== 4: too low error
== 5: too high error
== 6: too low warning
== 7: too high warning

To simulate this it requires an extra lookup table which is the inverse of the normal signal lookup table, it should transfor the real world value back into the raw input.</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Memory store/Diagnostic to memory/Diagnostic Helpers/Diagnostic simulation</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Memory store/Diagnostic to memory/Diagnostic Helpers/Diagnostic simulation</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>5  610  170  660</block_position><block_icon>Memory@20@store/Diagnostic@20@to@20@memory/Diagnostic@20@Helpers/Diagnostic@20@simulation.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4341" hasTree="0"><block_name>Generic diagnostic</block_name><block_class>Generic actuator diagnostic block</block_class><block_desc>A simple diagnostic helper, diagnostics start after "Start delay" and then if trigger == 1 for "Fault duration" it will write the fault code to memory.

Once the trigger drops to 0 again, the volatile output will also go back to 0, but the persistant output will remain at 1. Untill the reset input is applied, this will also reset the persistant output. Note that this will not delete the code from memory.

The naming parameter is non functional but helps by naming the output signals and being displayed in the mask.</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Memory store/Diagnostic to memory/Diagnostic Helpers/Generic diagnostic</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Memory store/Diagnostic to memory/Diagnostic Helpers/Generic diagnostic</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>-75  699  230  786</block_position><block_icon>Memory@20@store/Diagnostic@20@to@20@memory/Diagnostic@20@Helpers/Generic@20@diagnostic.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:2,signal.out_signal:2</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node></block_node><block_node SID="4296" hasTree="0"><block_name>Number of stored diagnostic codes</block_name><block_class>Read number of stored codes</block_class><block_desc>This function block reads the number of stored codes.

Sample time:
Execution period for the function block.
You might want to put this block in a triggered subsystem or a function call subsystem</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Memory store/Diagnostic to memory/Number of stored diagnostic codes</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Memory store/Diagnostic to memory/Number of stored diagnostic codes</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>600  413  840  457</block_position><block_icon>Memory@20@store/Diagnostic@20@to@20@memory/Number@20@of@20@stored@20@diagnostic@20@codes.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.out_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4294" hasTree="0"><block_name>Delete all codes for one group</block_name><block_class>Delete diagnostic codes from memory</block_class><block_desc>This function block deletes all stored codes.</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Memory store/Diagnostic to memory/Delete all codes for one group</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Memory store/Diagnostic to memory/Delete all codes for one group</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>600  495  840  530</block_position><block_icon>Memory@20@store/Diagnostic@20@to@20@memory/Delete@20@all@20@codes@20@for@20@one@20@group.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4295" hasTree="0"><block_name>Delete single DTC code</block_name><block_class>Delete a single DTC from memory</block_class><block_desc>This function block deletes one DTC from memory:

SPN number:
A code to identify which part has failed

FMI:
How the part has failed</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Memory store/Diagnostic to memory/Delete single DTC code</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Memory store/Diagnostic to memory/Delete single DTC code</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>605  571  840  624</block_position><block_icon>Memory@20@store/Diagnostic@20@to@20@memory/Delete@20@single@20@DTC@20@code.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:3</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node></block_node></block_node><block_node SID="4017" hasTree="0"><block_name>System</block_name><block_class></block_class><block_desc></block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/System</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/System</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>1</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>-330  -66 -230  -24</block_position><block_icon>System.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>NA.NA:0</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference><block_node SID="4100" hasTree="0"><block_name>Execute shell command</block_name><block_class>Execute shell command</block_class><block_desc>Execute a Linux Shell command when the input is true.

Accepts a Boolean input, use a triggered subsystem to call the function only once on a state change instead of every sample.&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;For the Linux controllers (Moduline M1, L4, HMI1) only.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/System/Execute shell command</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/System/Execute shell command</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>515   85  665  135</block_position><block_icon>System/Execute@20@shell@20@command.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4099" hasTree="0"><block_name>Controller temperature</block_name><block_class>Controller temperature</block_class><block_desc>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Controller Temperature&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
This block provides the internal controller temperature measured by the
integrated temperature sensor.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
The sensor is embedded inside the controller and reflects the internal
operating temperature of the device.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
The reported temperature does not represent ambient temperature, as it is
influenced by internal heat dissipation and system load.
&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/System/Controller temperature</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/System/Controller temperature</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>515  288  665  332</block_position><block_icon>System/Controller@20@temperature.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.out_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4352" hasTree="0"><block_name>Application free heap</block_name><block_class>Application free heap</block_class><block_desc>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Application Heap&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
This block provides information about the available heap memory of the
Moduline S1 application.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
The reported value reflects the remaining dynamic memory that can be
allocated during runtime.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
It can be used to monitor memory usage and to detect potential memory
exhaustion during development and operation.
&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/System/Application free heap</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/System/Application free heap</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>715  288  865  332</block_position><block_icon>System/Application@20@free@20@heap.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.out_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4351" hasTree="0"><block_name>Application available stack</block_name><block_class>Application available stack</block_class><block_desc>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Available Stack Monitor&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
This block provides the remaining available stack memory of the running application.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
It can be used to monitor stack usage during development and to detect potential
stack overflow conditions.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
This functionality is only available on Moduline S1 controllers.
&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/System/Application available stack</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/System/Application available stack</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>515  373  665  417</block_position><block_icon>System/Application@20@available@20@stack.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.out_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4353" hasTree="0"><block_name>Application CPU load</block_name><block_class>Application CPU load</block_class><block_desc>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Application CPU Load&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
This block provides the current CPU load of the Moduline S1 application.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
The reported value represents the processor usage of the running
application and can be used to monitor system performance.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
It is intended for diagnostic and performance analysis purposes during
development and runtime monitoring.
&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/System/Application CPU load</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/System/Application CPU load</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>715  373  865  417</block_position><block_icon>System/Application@20@CPU@20@load.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.out_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4228" hasTree="0"><block_name>XCP Server</block_name><block_class>XCP Server</block_class><block_desc>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;XCP Server&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
Adding one block of this type to the model enables the integrated XCP server,
allowing calibration and measurement via HANtune.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Notes&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
Only one XCP Server block should be present in the model.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
Moduline S1 supports XCP over CAN or over Bluetooth. Over Bluetooth the XCP
packets travel through the ESP32 to a host such as the GOcontroll Monitor;
the A2L file is the same.
&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/System/XCP Server</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/System/XCP Server</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>515  459  665  511</block_position><block_icon>System/XCP@20@Server.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>NA.NA:0</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4277" hasTree="0"><block_name>Software version</block_name><block_class>Software version</block_class><block_desc>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Software version&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
This block defines the software version of the model.
The version follows semantic versioning (&lt;b&gt;Major.Minor.Patch&lt;/b&gt;).
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
The three version numbers are available as output signals for use in the model,
for example via HANtune measurements or CAN messages.
They are also stored in the controller info for later retrieval.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Outputs&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Major&lt;/b&gt; &amp;mdash; Incremented on breaking changes or major new functionality.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Minor&lt;/b&gt; &amp;mdash; Incremented on new features, backwards compatible.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Patch&lt;/b&gt; &amp;mdash; Incremented on bug fixes, backwards compatible.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Notes&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
Only one Software version block should be present in the model.&lt;br/&gt;
Each version field must be an integer in the range 0&amp;ndash;255.
&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/System/Software version</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/System/Software version</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>505  560  675  610</block_position><block_icon>System/Software@20@version.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.out_signal:3</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4299" hasTree="0"><block_name>Read accelerometer</block_name><block_class>Read accelerometer</block_class><block_desc>This block reads out the accelerometer embedded into several Moduline controllers

Moduline M1 3 axis acceleration measurement
Moduline S1 3 axis acceleration measurement with 3 axis gyroscopes

It outputs the acceleration in all 3 spatial dimensons in m/s^2.

Positive X axis is acceleration towards the connectors in the length of the controller
Positive Y axis is acceleration towards the empty side of the controller, so opposite of the two m12 connectors
Positive Z axis is acceleration straight up if the controller was lying on its back so towards the indicator leds</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/System/Read accelerometer</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/System/Read accelerometer</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>505  641  670  759</block_position><block_icon>System/Read@20@accelerometer.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.out_signal:6</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4344" hasTree="0"><block_name>Read GPS</block_name><block_class>Read gps</block_class><block_desc>Reads several data points from an installed 4G/GPS module.

latitude/longitude in degrees
altitude in meters
velocity in meters per second

Southern hemisphere is negative latitude
Western hemisphere is negative longitude

The date/time is in UTC

All outputs are 0 untill a connection to a gps satelite is made.
When it fails to read a valid value from the gps module it also outputs all 0.</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/System/Read GPS</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/System/Read GPS</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>510  791  665  999</block_position><block_icon>System/Read@20@GPS.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.out_signal:11</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4401" hasTree="0"><block_name>Read RTC</block_name><block_class>Read gps</block_class><block_desc>Reads several data points from an installed 4G/GPS module.

latitude/longitude in degrees
altitude in meters
velocity in meters per second

Southern hemisphere is negative latitude
Western hemisphere is negative longitude

The date/time is in UTC

All outputs are 0 untill a connection to a gps satelite is made.
When it fails to read a valid value from the gps module it also outputs all 0.</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/System/Read RTC</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/System/Read RTC</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>750   793   905  1002</block_position><block_icon>System/Read@20@RTC.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.out_signal:6</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="4345" hasTree="0"><block_name>RAM configuration</block_name><block_class>RAM configuration</block_class><block_desc>Set the Stack size for the main thread and read available memory in the stack and heap.</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/System/RAM configuration</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/System/RAM configuration</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>510  1056   665  1129</block_position><block_icon>System/RAM@20@configuration.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.out_signal:2</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node></block_node><block_node SID="4089" hasTree="0"><block_name>Display specific</block_name><block_class></block_class><block_desc></block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Display specific</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Display specific</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>1</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>-330    5 -230   45</block_position><block_icon>Display@20@specific.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>NA.NA:0</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference><block_node SID="4098" hasTree="0"><block_name>Set display brightness</block_name><block_class>Set display brightness</block_class><block_desc>Set the brightness of a display (only works with the max25014 driver currently)
Keeps the original brightness untill the first non zero value is received&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;For the Linux controllers (Moduline M1, L4, HMI1) only.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Display specific/Set display brightness</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Display specific/Set display brightness</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>70  114  215  166</block_position><block_icon>Display@20@specific/Set@20@display@20@brightness.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>M-S-Function</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node></block_node></library>
